## Item text
Free newsletter

Rucking notes by email

New gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.

Three items cover the start: a sturdy backpack, a weight that can't shift (5-10 lb for a first ruck), and good socks.

Upgrade the part that rubs, shifts or no longer suits your use. Comfortable gear you already own can stay.

The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 is our pick for dedicated plate training, the 5.11 Rush 12 2.0 is the value pick, and any sturdy daypack is fine for starting.

Ruck plates are a convenience upgrade. Bricks in a towel train you just as well.

Start with what you own

A sturdy backpack, a manageable weight held securely, and comfortable shoes and socks cover a first ruck. Buy an upgrade when it solves a specific problem with fit, load movement or carrying the gear you need.

How we evaluate gear

Recommendations compare published specifications, plate retention, fit and warranty terms. Individual product reviews distinguish firsthand use from researched recommendations. Fit and comfort still need checking with your own load and clothing.

Some retailer links are affiliate links, which means Ruck Authority may earn a commission if you buy through them, at no extra cost to you. Recommendations don't change with commission rate.

Find your gear in 30 seconds

Answer five quick questions about your budget, primary use, terrain, weight range, and body type, and get personalized gear recommendations. The quiz considers your actual constraints (budget matters), your goals (fitness rucking is different from event prep), and your environment (trail-capable packs are different from road packs).

Rucksacks: the complete buyer's guide

What makes a good rucking pack

Any sturdy backpack works for your first rucks. Once you're carrying a dense weight several times a week, these are the features that make a pack carry better:

Frame sheet or internal frame. The weight needs to stay pressed against your spine without sagging. A frame sheet stops the load from bulging away from your back and pulling on your shoulders, and it matters more as the weight goes up.

Ruck plate pocket or sleeve. A dedicated pocket holds a plate high and tight against your back so it can't slide around inside the pack. Without one, you can still pad a plate against the back panel, but it takes more care to keep it from shifting. If you want purpose-built options, start with the best weighted backpacks for rucking.

Hip belt, built in or optional. A hip belt moves part of the load from your shoulders to your hips, which helps on longer, heavier rucks. Many training rucks, including GORUCK's, treat it as an add-on and are designed to be carried on the shoulders by default.

Padded shoulder straps with a sternum strap. The shoulder straps carry most of the load, so they need real padding and a shape that doesn't dig in. A sternum strap across your chest keeps the straps from sliding outward.

Durable fabric. Heavy nylon such as 1000D Cordura is the common choice for dedicated rucks, because a hard plate wears through thin fabric over time. Lighter ripstop is fine for lighter loads.

Appropriate volume. For fitness rucking, 20-25 liters is plenty. Larger packs let a plate sit lower and add fabric weight you don't need. For events or longer days out, 25-35 liters gives you room for water, layers and food.

Best rucksacks by category

The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 is the current purpose-built training pack in the line. Its two elevated pockets hold Standard and Long plate formats close to the upper back, while the lumbar pad and external handles support loaded miles and ruck PT. Check the current GORUCK lineup guide when work, travel, or a lower entry price also belong in the decision.

The downside is cost and specialization. It doesn't have the laptop compartment that makes the GR1 useful for work, and beginners staying at or under a 30 lb Standard plate may not need more than the Basic Rucker.

The 5.11 Rush 12 2.0 is a tough 24L pack in 1050D water-repellent nylon with contoured yoke straps, at a much lower price than a GORUCK Rucker. It has no frame sheet, hip belt or dedicated plate pocket, but its hydration pocket (listed at 16.5 x 10 inches) is wide enough to hold a 9 x 11.5 inch Standard plate against the back panel.

That makes it a good value pick for light-to-moderate loads. As the weight climbs past what an unframed pack carries comfortably, a purpose-built ruck with a frame sheet and elevated plate pocket earns its price.

See our GORUCK alternatives comparison for more options across price points.

On average, women have shorter torsos and narrower shoulders than men, and many unisex packs are sized around male proportions, which can leave the straps sitting wide or the pack riding low.

Look for a pack size and shoulder-strap shape that match the actual torso. GORUCK offers several capacities, but they are not a substitute for trying the loaded fit. There are also women-specific options with narrower harness geometry.

See our best rucking gear for women guide for fit-specific recommendations for packs, vests, and shoes.

You don't need a fancy ruck pack to start. A regular backpack (even a school backpack or daypack) works for your first rucks, as long as it has two padded shoulder straps and holds the weight close to your back.

Pair it with DIY weight (bricks, water, sand) wrapped or bagged so it can't shift, and you have a working rucking setup for very little money.

Our budget rucking starter kit shows how far you can get before spending on dedicated gear.

Ruck plates and weight options

Ruck plates

A ruck plate is a flat, dense weight designed to fit into the ruck plate pocket of a backpack. Think of it as a rectangle of metal or composite material that locks weight against your spine.

Advantages: Ruck plates are compact and stable, and a plate in a well-fitted pocket won't shift during movement. Common weights are 10, 20, 30 and 45 lb. GORUCK's 10, 20 and 30 lb Standard plates share a 9 x 11.5 inch footprint and get thicker as they get heavier; its 30 and 45 lb Long plates measure 9.5 x 15 inches.

Materials: Most ruck plates are cast or ductile iron with a painted or powder coating. GORUCK's are powder-coated ductile iron. A coating that stays intact keeps rust off, and a thin, flat profile matters more for comfort than the metal itself.

Cost: Plates cost more than DIY weight, and brand-name plates cost noticeably more than generic cast iron. The product cards show current price bands.

The plate-of-record for the GORUCK Rucker pocket is GORUCK's own Standard plate, sized to the pocket so it doesn't shift. The budget swap is a generic cast-iron ruck weight such as Yes4All's: check its dimensions against your pack's pocket before buying, because generic plates don't all match GORUCK's footprint.

For a detailed comparison of ruck plates from different manufacturers, including how each fits common packs, see Ruck Plate Showdown.

Sandbag fillers

Sandbag fillers are cheaper than ruck plates and moldable. You can fill them to any weight you like, which is particularly useful for beginners: you can start light and progress without buying multiple plates.

A good sandbag filler (canvas or nylon, around 8"x12") is inexpensive and can be filled with sand or pea gravel from any hardware store.

The downside: sandbag fillers can shift inside your pack unless they're packed tightly, and they don't last as long as ruck plates.

DIY options

Bricks, water bottles and dumbbells all work for starting. A standard brick weighs roughly 4 to 5 pounds, and water is about 8.3 pounds per gallon. Wrap dumbbells or bricks in a towel so hard edges don't dig into your back.

DIY is great for trying rucking without spending money, but it's not ideal long-term because the weight distribution is uneven and the materials aren't designed for the purpose.

Shoes for rucking

A pack adds weight to every step, so a shoe that stays stable under load matters more than it does on an ordinary walk. There's little research on rucking footwear specifically, and fit and comfort decide most of it. Many people start in the walking or running shoes they already own; the ones that tend to struggle under a heavier pack are very soft, unstable models and minimalist shoes with little underfoot.

A stable, well-fitting shoe with a firm enough sole is the practical target. Heel-to-toe drop is the height difference between your heel and your forefoot. Many ruckers prefer a moderate drop, but there's no research-backed ideal number, so choose by comfort.

Trail runners, hiking shoes and supportive walking shoes all work. A Salomon trail runner or a Merrell hiking shoe is a typical example: a stable sole, enough cushioning and a durable upper.

Our pick across mixed terrain is the Salomon XA Pro 3D V9 GTX: an aggressive Contagrip outsole, an 11mm drop and GORE-TEX waterproofing for wet routes. For pavement-heavy training, the Hoka Bondi 9 is a max-cushion road alternative worth trying if firmer shoes leave your feet or knees sore.

For terrain-specific shoe picks and reviews, read our best rucking shoes by terrain guide. We rank shoes by pavement, gravel, trail, and mixed terrain so you can pick by the surfaces you actually ruck on.

Accessories that actually matter

Some accessories earn their place in the pack and some don't. Spend on the first group and skip the last.

Good socks. Keep comfortable, well-fitting athletic socks you already own, including moisture-wicking synthetics. If your current pair stays damp, bunches or rubs, Darn Tough Light Hiker merino socks are an option to try, backed by Darn Tough's lifetime guarantee. Merino isn't a requirement to start.

Water bottle or hydration bladder. A Nalgene Wide-Mouth 32 oz bottle on a loop, or a Source WXP 3L bladder with a drinking tube. Dehydration hurts performance and raises the risk of heat illness on warm days. Compare our verified hydration bladder picks before choosing a reservoir.

Anti-chafe. Body Glide Original on the inner thighs and shoulder-strap contact points. Apply it before you set off.

GPS watch with HR tracking. The Garmin Instinct 3 Solar is our pick: a rugged case, long solar-assisted battery life, heart-rate zone alerts and sleep tracking. It's useful once you're rucking several times a week and want to see the pattern.

Headlamp. For early-morning or evening rucks, the current Petzl Actik Core reaches 625 lumens with its included USB-C rechargeable CORE battery. The full breakdown is in our best headlamps for rucking guide.

Reflective vest or gear. On roads in low light, a reflective vest or ankle bands are cheap and important for safety.

Rucking-specific gloves. Regular work gloves or no gloves work fine unless you're regularly below 20°F.

A GPS watch on day one. Your phone already tracks distance and pace. Add a watch later if you want heart-rate data or plan to train for events.

Compression gear. The evidence that compression garments help performance is weak, any recovery benefit in the research is small, and none of it is specific to rucking. Skip it unless you like how it feels.

Gear by budget tier

These are equipment choices, not training stages. Keep your current repeatable load and schedule whichever setup you buy; a bigger budget doesn't establish readiness for more weight or more sessions.

Read the full breakdown: the budget rucking starter kit.

How to maintain your gear

Rucksack: Hand wash with mild soap and water rather than putting it in the machine. Cordura holds up for many years if you don't shred it. Air dry completely before storing. Now and then, refresh the Durable Water Repellent (DWR) coating with an inexpensive spray-on product.

Ruck plates: Wipe down after dusty or muddy rucks. Painted cast-iron plates can rust where the paint chips, so check them occasionally and store them dry. Powder-coated plates rarely rust while the coating is intact, but a wipe-down still helps.

Shoes: Remove insoles after wet rucks and air dry (never use heat). Rotating two pairs lets each dry out fully between rucks. Replace shoes when the midsole looks creased or the shoe starts to feel flat; a loaded pack won't make the cushioning last longer than it does for running.

The best gear is the gear you'll actually use consistently. An expensive rucksack collecting dust is worth less than an old backpack with bricks in it that you carry three times a week. Start cheap, build the habit, and upgrade when the gear becomes the limiting factor.

